1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,688
Lesson 3.3.6: Falls and Provider Visits.

2
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,400
This lesson covers two more sections of the problem note.

3
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:17,767
Falls first: whether the patient has a history of falls. The MDS,

4
00:00:17,807 --> 00:00:22,761
a fall risk evaluation, and the progress notes can all tell us.

5
00:00:22,811 --> 00:00:26,668
Then the fall risk score, which comes from the Assessments tab.

6
00:00:26,718 --> 00:00:29,238
And provider visits related to the condition:

7
00:00:29,278 --> 00:00:34,107
for a scratch patient, every related visit in the past three months.

8
00:00:34,157 --> 00:00:39,092
For a follow-up patient, every related visit since the last date of service.

9
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:47,039
The falls section records two things: whether the patient has a history of falls,

10
00:00:47,079 --> 00:00:50,189
and the fall risk score, with what it means.

11
00:00:50,239 --> 00:00:52,629
Three places in PointClickCare can tell us:

12
00:00:52,669 --> 00:00:57,075
the MDS, the Assessments tab, and the progress notes.

13
00:01:00,667 --> 00:01:05,516
The MDS tab comes first. It lists the patient's MDS assessments,

14
00:01:05,556 --> 00:01:08,414
and we want the last completed one.

15
00:01:10,067 --> 00:01:14,856
Here, that's the annual assessment from November 10, 2026.

16
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,590
Print opens it as a PDF,

17
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:23,810
and searching the PDF for falls leads to question J1800.

18
00:01:26,733 --> 00:01:29,166
Has the resident had any falls since admission,

19
00:01:29,206 --> 00:01:34,029
entry or reentry, or the prior assessment? The code is 1.

20
00:01:34,079 --> 00:01:37,844
And 1 means yes: the patient has a history of falls.

21
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:44,528
The fall risk score comes from the Assessments tab.

22
00:01:46,167 --> 00:01:49,066
With View All, and the status and type filters on All,

23
00:01:49,107 --> 00:01:51,295
every assessment is listed.

24
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:02,431
A search for fall finds the Fall Risk Evaluation from October 21, 2026.

25
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:10,512
Scrolling right shows its score without even opening it: 15,

26
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:12,759
which means the patient is at risk.

27
00:02:17,533 --> 00:02:21,049
Print, next to the evaluation, opens it in full.

28
00:02:23,233 --> 00:02:26,842
Its first item is the history of falls in the past three months,

29
00:02:26,882 --> 00:02:29,619
and the answer is one to two falls.

30
00:02:29,669 --> 00:02:35,220
So this evaluation agrees with the MDS: the patient has a history of falls.

31
00:02:38,867 --> 00:02:44,042
It was completed on October 21, while the MDS is from November 10.

32
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:53,636
If the two ever disagree, the most recent one is the one we go by.

33
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:58,257
When neither the MDS nor the assessments answer the question,

34
00:02:58,297 --> 00:03:01,424
the progress notes are the last place to look.

35
00:03:04,133 --> 00:03:08,273
A search for fall finds the monthly evaluation from November 10:

36
00:03:08,314 --> 00:03:11,819
falls since last evaluation, yes.

37
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:16,216
So there are three places to check, in this order,

38
00:03:16,256 --> 00:03:20,289
and the most recent information is what goes into the note.

39
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:25,450
The note says: the patient has a history of falls,

40
00:03:25,490 --> 00:03:31,443
and the fall risk score was 15 on October 21, 2026.

41
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:35,720
Next is provider visits related to the condition.

42
00:03:36,833 --> 00:03:38,580
For a scratch patient like ours,

43
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:42,046
that's every related visit in the past three months.

44
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:46,859
For a follow-up patient, it's every related visit since the last date of service:

45
00:03:46,899 --> 00:03:49,242
the date of our last follow-up.

46
00:03:51,833 --> 00:03:55,798
In the progress notes, a custom effective date range sets the window:

47
00:03:55,838 --> 00:04:00,398
from August 25 until today, November 25.

48
00:04:12,833 --> 00:04:17,544
Searching for the word signed finds every signed provider note in that window.

49
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:20,985
There are 30 matches, and each note shows the word twice,

50
00:04:21,025 --> 00:04:23,312
so that's about 15 notes.

51
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:32,789
For each note, two things matter. First, the date of the visit,

52
00:04:32,829 --> 00:04:37,697
at the top of the note: here, November 10, 2026.

53
00:04:44,133 --> 00:04:45,860
Second, the assessment and plan:

54
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:49,446
what the provider assessed, and what they recommended.

55
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:55,194
This provider assessed hypothyroidism, dementia and depression,

56
00:04:55,234 --> 00:04:59,891
diabetes, and COPD, but not hyperlipidemia.

57
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:04,811
So this visit isn't related to our condition.

58
00:05:08,767 --> 00:05:11,782
The October 20 visit covers knee pain and falls,

59
00:05:11,822 --> 00:05:14,525
with nothing for hyperlipidemia.

60
00:05:20,833 --> 00:05:25,423
And the October 7 visit covers hypertension and hypothyroidism:

61
00:05:25,463 --> 00:05:27,266
not related either.

62
00:05:35,833 --> 00:05:39,094
The rest of the notes in the window are the same story.

63
00:05:41,133 --> 00:05:42,530
So in the past three months,

64
00:05:42,570 --> 00:05:46,523
the patient has no visits related to hyperlipidemia.

65
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:58,386
And the note says: no recent visits related to hyperlipidemia.

66
00:06:00,767 --> 00:06:02,747
But when a related visit does exist,

67
00:06:02,787 --> 00:06:07,680
the Templates tab in the CCM Clinical Tool has a template for it.

68
00:06:12,833 --> 00:06:16,593
Say the condition were dementia, and a visit had assessed it.

69
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:25,658
We'd document only the part of the assessment and plan about that condition,

70
00:06:29,533 --> 00:06:33,240
the provider who saw the patient, named at the end of the note,

71
00:06:37,133 --> 00:06:40,202
and the date of service, from the beginning of the note.

72
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:47,126
In the template: the patient was seen by the provider on the date of service,

73
00:06:47,166 --> 00:06:50,733
and the plan was as follows, then the plan itself.

74
00:06:56,100 --> 00:06:58,014
Here, no related visit turned up,

75
00:06:58,054 --> 00:07:01,856
so the note simply says: no recent provider visits.

76
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:14,821
To wrap up: the MDS answers question J1800, any falls since the last assessment.

77
00:07:14,871 --> 00:07:17,688
The fall risk score comes from the Assessments tab:

78
00:07:17,728 --> 00:07:19,968
scrolling right shows the score.

79
00:07:20,018 --> 00:07:23,054
When sources disagree, the most recent one counts,

80
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:25,543
and the progress notes come last.

81
00:07:25,593 --> 00:07:28,493
Provider visits cover three months for a scratch patient,

82
00:07:28,533 --> 00:07:32,645
or the time since the last date of service for a follow-up.

83
00:07:32,695 --> 00:07:35,337
A search for signed finds the provider notes,

84
00:07:35,377 --> 00:07:40,234
and each one's date and assessment and plan tell us if it's related.

85
00:07:40,284 --> 00:07:43,374
And a related visit is documented with the template:

86
00:07:43,414 --> 00:07:46,741
the provider, the date of service, and the plan.

87
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:51,313
Next come the last sections of the problem note:

88
00:07:51,353 --> 00:07:58,246
hospitalizations and ER visits, referrals, medication changes, and escalation.
